---
import Video from '../../components/story/Video.astro';
import Zoom from '../../components/story/Zoom.astro';
import tokensChart from '../../assets/projects/matrix-claude-code-tokens-0.2.9.png';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import '../../styles/story.css';
---
<Base wide title="Matrix for Claude Code: 5.6× fewer input tokens" image="/og-matrix-claude-code.png" description="A free plugin that gives Claude Code a local, searchable index of PDFs, Word files, scans and charts. Measured on 442 pages: 5.6× fewer input tokens, 2.4× fewer output tokens, 20 instead of 48 s.">
<div class="story">

  <section class="beat" style="border-top:0">
    <div class="in">
      <Video src="/video/matrix-claude-code-1080p.mp4" poster="/video/matrix-claude-code-poster.jpg" caption="Recorded in October with an earlier plugin version; the numbers in the video are out of date. The current ones are from November 2026, version 0.2.9." />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="beat" style="border-top:0">
    <div class="in">
      <p class="kicker">Matrix for Claude Code</p>
      <h1>Claude Code needed 5.6× fewer input tokens.</h1>
      <p class="big">With this free plugin, Claude Code used 182k instead of 1,025k input tokens per question on 442 pages
      of EU regulations and a report, and it wrote 2.4× fewer output tokens. No loss in quality: all 8 answers were
      right, against 7 of 8 without it.</p>
      <p class="muted">Fewer tokens mean your subscription limit lasts longer, the API bill is smaller, and Claude's
      context window keeps room for your work. It was also faster: 20 instead of 48 seconds per question.</p>
      <p class="muted">The plugin gives Claude Code a local index of your PDFs, Word files, scans and charts. Every answer
      comes with file, page and passage. Claude Code is the language model, so you need no extra model and no API key.</p>
      <a class="go" href="https://github.com/matthiasnordwig/matrix-mcp">Get it on GitHub</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="beat alt">
    <div class="in split">
      <div><p class="kicker">The problem</p><h2>Without an index, every question starts from zero.</h2></div>
      <div>
        <p class="big">Claude Code converts the PDFs to text, greps, opens pages and greps again. That works, but on a few
        hundred pages it takes more than a dozen tool calls per question.</p>
        <p class="muted">Matrix reads the documents once. It splits them by their structure, indexes them and gives
        Claude Code short excerpts of the matching sections instead of whole documents.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="beat">
    <div class="in">
      <p class="kicker">What it does</p>
      <h2>It finds the passage and the page.</h2>
      <div class="grid3">
        <div><h3>Answers with sources</h3><p>Every answer names file, page and passage. The citations are checked against the original text.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Tables across documents</h3><p>One question for every document or passage, returned as a table with a source in each cell.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Other words, other languages</h3><p>Search works on meaning, so an English question finds the German provision.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Scans and charts</h3><p>Scanned pages are searchable. A value that exists only in a chart is found on its page.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Cross-references</h3><p>"Pursuant to Article 26" leads to Article 26, and Claude can read the whole article, not just a snippet.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Stays current</h3><p>New and changed files in the folder are indexed again automatically. Deleted files drop out of the search.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="beat alt">
    <div class="in">
      <p class="kicker">The measurement</p>
      <h2>Same questions, with and without the plugin.</h2>
      <p class="big">Claude Code alone against Claude Code with the plugin, on 442 pages of public EU regulations and a
      report. Same questions, same prompt, two runs each. I wrote down the right answers before the first run.</p>
      <div class="numbers">
        <div><strong>5.6× fewer</strong><span>input tokens per question: 182k instead of 1,025k</span></div>
        <div><strong>2.4× fewer</strong><span>output tokens: 1.6k instead of 3.9k</span></div>
        <div><strong>45 % fewer</strong><span>tool calls: 7.9 instead of 14.4</span></div>
        <div><strong>2.4× faster</strong><span>20 s instead of 48 s per question, a side effect of reading less</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="chart shot"><Zoom id="z-tokens" src={tokensChart} alt="Bar chart titled: Claude Code on 442 pages of PDFs, 5.6× fewer input tokens. Average per question, Sonnet, plugin 0.2.9, November 2026, two runs per question each; Claude Code alone against Claude Code with the Matrix plugin. Input tokens: English question on German legal text 851k to 172k, value only in a chart 913k to 232k, answer spread over three regulations 1,415k to 223k, paraphrased question on one regulation 920k to 102k, average 1,025k to 182k. Output tokens, 2.4× fewer, same order: 3.1k to 1.1k, 3.3k to 1.5k, 6.4k to 3.0k, 2.9k to 0.8k, average 3.9k to 1.6k. Time, shown as a side effect, same order: 38 to 21 s, 43 to 21 s, 71 to 27 s, 38 to 12 s, average 48 to 20 s. Footnote: same documents, questions and answers as in October, when it was 651k input tokens alone and 303k with the plugin." caption="Input tokens, output tokens and time. Each bar is the average of two runs per question." /></div>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="demo">
          <thead><tr><th>Question (442 pages)</th><th>Alone</th><th>With Matrix</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>English question, German legal text</td><td>851k in · 3.1k out · 38 s</td><td class="ok">172k in · 1.1k out · 21 s</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A number that exists only in a chart</td><td>913k in · 3.3k out · 43 s</td><td class="ok">232k in · 1.5k out · 21 s</td></tr>
            <tr><td>One answer spread over three regulations</td><td>1,415k in · 6.4k out · 71 s</td><td class="ok">223k in · 3.0k out · 27 s</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Paraphrased question, single regulation</td><td>920k in · 2.9k out · 38 s</td><td class="ok">102k in · 0.8k out · 12 s</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="muted" style="margin-top:1.5rem">Averages per run, Claude Code 2.1.309 with Sonnet and plugin 0.2.9, November 2026.
      Two runs per question on both sides. Input tokens add up what the model processes over all turns; output tokens are what it writes, its tool calls and the answer. Cache reads, the same conversation sent again with each turn, make up 93 % of them without the plugin and 86 % with it; without cache reads it is 76k against 25k tokens. With the plugin all 8 answers were right; without it 7 of 8, because Claude Code once read a value from the wrong chart. The second run without the plugin ran while another build used the Mac, so its times may be slightly high; the tokens are unaffected. The questions, the right answers
      and the script to run it again are in <a href="https://github.com/matthiasnordwig/matrix-mcp/blob/main/DEMO.md">DEMO.md</a>.</p>
      <p class="muted">A second, larger test set was measured in October with plugin version 0.2.3, one run per question:
      on 1,592 pages in 14 documents and four languages, the plugin used 34 % fewer input tokens and 35 % less time.</p>
      <p class="muted">The first run of this test, in October with Claude Code 2.1.222 and an earlier plugin version, gave 303k
      instead of 651k tokens and 42 instead of 126 seconds. Without the plugin, Claude Code now needs more input tokens,
      1,025k instead of 651k, but much less time, 48 instead of 126 seconds. So the plugin now saves more than four fifths of the input
      tokens instead of about half, and a bit more than half the time instead of two thirds.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="beat">
    <div class="in">
      <p class="kicker">The limits</p>
      <h2>It saves tokens and time. Better answers are not proven.</h2>
      <p class="big">Claude Code alone got 7 of 8 answers right. One wrong answer in eight runs is not an error rate.</p>
      <div class="grid3">
        <div><h3>More documents, more gain</h3><p>Here the paraphrased question on one regulation saved the largest share (920k → 102k tokens), the one spread over three regulations the most tokens (1,415k → 223k). For one short document the gain is small.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Spread-out questions: mixed</h3><p>On the larger set, in October with version 0.2.3, a question across three acts saved almost nothing, because Claude still searches once per act. On the 442 pages in November it was the other way round: 1,415k → 223k tokens, 71 → 27 s.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Indexing takes minutes</h3><p>Once per folder: 16 minutes for 1,592 pages, with no Claude tokens. It pays off if you ask more than once.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Page numbers slipped</h3><p>In the October test of version 0.2.3, two citations were one page off where a passage crosses a page break. File, article and passage were right in every answer. Fixed in the released 0.2.3: a hit lists every page its passage covers.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Charts are easy to misread</h3><p>Without the plugin, in one of two runs, Claude Code took 15.87 % from another chart (p. 13) in a low-resolution page image, instead of 19.01 % on p. 10. In October the plugin had the same problem once, because the page image was too small. This time it found and read the chart page right in both runs.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Small samples</h3><p>Two runs per question on both sides on 442 pages in November, one run on 1,592 pages in October. Without the plugin, the two runs of one question differed by up to 56 % in tokens (665k and 1,037k).</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="beat alt">
    <div class="in">
      <p class="kicker">How it works</p>
      <h2>Code finds the passages. Claude reads them.</h2>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li><div><h3>Split along the real structure <span class="who rules">rules</span></h3><p>By PDF layout, headings, articles and tables. No language model decides where a section ends. Scans and charts are indexed too.</p></div></li>
        <li><div><h3>Search by meaning and by keyword <span class="who rules">rules</span></h3><p>Vectors from a multilingual embedding model plus full-text search, merged by rank. The vector database is built in and runs on SQLite: nothing to install, no server. An optional second model re-sorts the top hits.</p></div></li>
        <li><div><h3>Short hits <span class="who rules">rules</span></h3><p>A search returns as many short excerpts as fit into one result, each with file, pages and the path document › chapter › article.</p></div></li>
        <li><div><h3>Read before citing <span class="who ai">AI</span></h3><p>Claude Code opens the passage or the article it wants to quote. Then it answers with file, page and passage.</p></div></li>
        <li><div><h3>Check the citations <span class="who rules">rules</span></h3><p>Each citation is checked against the original text.</p></div></li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="beat">
    <div class="in split">
      <div><p class="kicker">Installation</p><h2>Two commands, then ask.</h2></div>
      <div>
        <div class="card"><pre><code>claude plugin marketplace add matthiasnordwig/matrix-mcp
claude plugin install matrix@matrix-mcp</code></pre></div>
        <p class="muted">Start Claude Code in the folder with your documents and ask. The first time, it walks you
        through the setup. It runs on macOS (Apple silicon), Windows 10 or later (x64) and Linux (x64 and arm64), with Node.js 18 or later.
        If it does not run on your system, <a href="mailto:matthias.nordwig@programmiere.de?subject=Matrix%20for%20Claude%20Code">tell me</a>.</p>
        <p class="muted">Matrix reads the folder you started in and asks before it opens any other. It never changes or
        deletes your files. The only text that leaves your computer is what Claude Code reads anyway.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="beat alt">
    <div class="in read">
      <p class="kicker">Where it comes from</p>
      <h2>It started as an app.</h2>
      <p class="big">The plugin runs on the same Rust core as <a href="/projects/matrix/">Matrix</a>, my document app for Mac,
      Windows and iPhone. In the app a local model does the reading; here Claude Code does it.</p>
      <a class="go" href="https://github.com/matthiasnordwig/matrix-mcp">Get it on GitHub</a>
      <a class="go ghost" href="mailto:matthias.nordwig@programmiere.de?subject=Matrix%20for%20Claude%20Code">Write to me</a>
    </div>
  </section>

</div>
</Base>

<style>
  .chart { max-width: 40rem; }
  /* Comparisons like "5.6× fewer" are longer than a plain number: keep them on one line in a four-tile row. */
  .story .numbers strong { font-size: clamp(2.2rem, 3.4vw, 2.9rem); }
  .chart + .table-wrap { margin-top: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3rem); }
</style>
